---
import Layout from '../components/Layout.astro';
import { BASE_OPTIONS, binaryHexGroups, convertBase, renderBaseLesson } from '../lib/bases';
import '../styles/bases.css';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const defaultLesson = convertBase('42', 10, 2);
const hexDigits = Array.from({ length: 16 }, (_, value) => ({
  value,
  hex: value.toString(16).toUpperCase(),
  bits: value.toString(2).padStart(4, '0'),
}));
const byteExample = binaryHexGroups(173n);
---

<Layout
  title="Understanding bases — Ancient Numbers"
  description="Learn place value, convert between bases with worked steps, and explore decimal, binary, hexadecimal, Maya base 20, and Babylonian base 60."
  active="bases"
>
  <div class="container bases-page">
    <header class="bases-hero">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow bases-kicker">A GUIDE TO NUMBER BASES</span>
        <h1>
          One number.
          <br />
          <em>Many bases.</em>
        </h1>
        <p>
          Forty-two things stay forty-two things. A base changes how we group and write that
          quantity. Learn the pattern behind place value, then follow a conversion one step at a
          time.
        </p>
        <nav class="bases-jump-links" aria-label="On this page">
          <a href="#place-value">Place value</a>
          <a href="#change-base">Change base</a>
          <a href="#why-bases">Why different bases?</a>
          <a href="#binary-hex">Binary &amp; hex</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
      <div class="bases-same-number">
        <span class="eyebrow">FORTY-TWO, IN EVERY ROW</span>
        <dl>
          <div>
            <dt>
              Decimal <small>BASE 10</small>
            </dt>
            <dd>42</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>
              Binary <small>BASE 2</small>
            </dt>
            <dd>101010</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>
              Hexadecimal <small>BASE 16</small>
            </dt>
            <dd>2A</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>
              Vigesimal <small>BASE 20</small>
            </dt>
            <dd>2,2</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>
              Sexagesimal <small>BASE 60</small>
            </dt>
            <dd>42</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p>Commas separate place digits in bases 20 and 60 on this page.</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section
      class="bases-section bases-foundations"
      id="place-value"
      aria-labelledby="place-heading"
    >
      <div class="bases-section-intro">
        <span class="eyebrow bases-kicker">01 · THE GROUPING RULE</span>
        <h2 id="place-heading">What does a base do?</h2>
        <p>
          In a positional number system, the <strong>base</strong> tells you how many units make one
          unit of the next place. In base 10, ten ones make a ten, and ten tens make a hundred. In
          base 2, two ones make a two, and two twos make a four.
        </p>
        <p>
          The rightmost whole-number place is always worth 1. Each step left multiplies that value
          by the base: 1, base, base², base³, and so on. A digit tells you how many of that place
          you have. Zero holds an empty place.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="base-place-example">
        <h3>Read 101010 in base 2</h3>
        <div
          class="base-table-scroll"
          tabindex="0"
          role="region"
          aria-label="Binary place-value example"
        >
          <table class="base-place-table">
            <caption>Each step left doubles the place value</caption>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Place value</th>
                {[32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1].map((n) => (
                  <td>{n}</td>
                ))}
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Digit</th>
                {[1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0].map((n) => (
                  <td class:list={{ 'base-digit-on': n === 1 }}>{n}</td>
                ))}
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <th scope="row">Contribution</th>
                {[32, 0, 8, 0, 2, 0].map((n) => (
                  <td>{n}</td>
                ))}
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p class="base-place-sum">
          32 + 8 + 2 = <strong>42</strong>
        </p>
        <p>
          Each digit must be smaller than its base. Binary has 0 and 1; after 1 comes 10, meaning
          one two and zero ones. So <strong>10 does not always mean ten</strong>.
        </p>
        <a class="bases-source" href="https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/binary-code2/en/">
          NASA · Place value in binary ↗
        </a>
      </div>
      <aside class="bases-notation-note">
        <h3>A base and a set of symbols are different ideas</h3>
        <p>
          Egyptian hieroglyphic numerals group by tens, with separate signs for 1, 10, 100, and so
          on. Their values come from the signs themselves, rather than a digit’s position. Roman
          numerals also use signs with fixed values, such as I, V, and X, with rules for combining
          them. Neither works like a row of positional decimal digits.
        </p>
        <p>
          Changing the base explains a number’s grouping. To learn how people actually added,
          subtracted, multiplied, or divided, choose an operator in the{' '}
          <a href={`${base}#converter`}>calculator</a>.
        </p>
        <div class="bases-note-links">
          <a href="https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Egyptian_numerals/">
            MacTutor · Egyptian numerals ↗
          </a>
          <a href={`${base}systems/roman/`}>Explore Roman numerals →</a>
        </div>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <section
      class="bases-section bases-converter"
      id="change-base"
      aria-labelledby="change-heading"
    >
      <div class="bases-section-intro">
        <span class="eyebrow bases-kicker">02 · TRY A CONVERSION</span>
        <h2 id="change-heading">Change the writing, keep the value.</h2>
        <p>
          First read the source digits using their place values. Then divide by the target base to
          find its digits. These are general conversion methods; individual civilizations had their
          own arithmetic practices.
        </p>
      </div>
      <noscript>
        <p class="bases-nojs">
          The worked example below is 42 in decimal converted to binary. Enable JavaScript to try
          your own number.
        </p>
      </noscript>
      <form id="base-converter-form" class="base-converter-form" data-base-interactive hidden>
        <div class="base-field base-number-field">
          <label for="base-number">Number</label>
          <input
            id="base-number"
            name="number"
            type="text"
            inputmode="numeric"
            value="42"
            maxlength="128"
            autocomplete="off"
            spellcheck="false"
            aria-describedby="base-input-help base-input-error"
          />
        </div>
        <div class="base-field">
          <label for="base-source">From base</label>
          <select id="base-source" name="from">
            {BASE_OPTIONS.map((option) => (
              <option value={option.value} selected={option.value === 10}>
                {option.name}
              </option>
            ))}
          </select>
        </div>
        <div class="base-field">
          <label for="base-target">To base</label>
          <select id="base-target" name="to">
            {BASE_OPTIONS.map((option) => (
              <option value={option.value} selected={option.value === 2}>
                {option.name}
              </option>
            ))}
          </select>
        </div>
        <button class="base-convert-button" type="submit">
          Show conversion <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </button>
        <p id="base-input-help" class="base-input-help">
          Use 0–9. Example: 42. Up to 16 place digits; nonnegative whole numbers only.
        </p>
        <p id="base-input-error" class="base-input-error" hidden></p>
      </form>
      <div class="base-presets" data-base-interactive hidden>
        <span>Try an example</span>
        <button type="button" data-base-example="10101101" data-source="2" data-target="16" hidden>
          Binary → hex
        </button>
        <button type="button" data-base-example="2A" data-source="16" data-target="10" hidden>
          Hex → decimal
        </button>
        <button type="button" data-base-example="1,12" data-source="60" data-target="10" hidden>
          Base 60 → decimal
        </button>
      </div>
      <p class="base-delimiter-note">
        <strong>For bases 20 and 60:</strong> type each place digit as a decimal number, separated
        by commas. For example, 1,12 in base 60 means 1 × 60 + 12 = 72. The comma is a place
        separator, never a thousands separator here. Spaces around commas are allowed. Fractions,
        signs, prefixes, colons, and semicolons are outside this lesson.
      </p>
      <div id="base-lesson" set:html={renderBaseLesson(defaultLesson)} />
      <p
        class="sr-only"
        id="base-announcement"
        role="status"
        aria-live="polite"
        aria-atomic="true"
      ></p>
    </section>

    <section class="bases-section" id="why-bases" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
      <div class="bases-section-intro">
        <span class="eyebrow bases-kicker">03 · PEOPLE, TOOLS &amp; TRADITION</span>
        <h2 id="why-heading">Why use different bases?</h2>
        <p>
          Number systems grow from counting habits, language, measurement, and the tools people use.
          A useful convention can be passed down for centuries. We often know how a system worked
          more confidently than why its earliest users settled on a particular grouping.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="bases-culture-grid">
        <article>
          <span class="bases-culture-number" aria-hidden="true">
            10
          </span>
          <h3>Decimal · hands and habit</h3>
          <p>
            Ten fingers provide a convenient counting aid, a plausible reason for the widespread use
            of tens. That is not proof of one shared origin for every decimal tradition. Egypt’s
            grouping by tens and modern decimal place value use different writing rules.
          </p>
          <a
            class="bases-source"
            href="https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Babylonian_numerals/"
          >
            MacTutor · The question of number bases ↗
          </a>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="bases-culture-number" aria-hidden="true">
            20
          </span>
          <h3>Vigesimal · larger body groupings</h3>
          <p>
            Counting fingers and toes gives twenty, a common explanation for vigesimal grouping.
            Treat it as an explanation, rather than a proven origin story for the Maya. Maya
            numerals use dots, bars, and zero signs and supported trade, astronomy, and calendar
            work.
          </p>
          <p>
            Here base 20 means pure powers of twenty. The{' '}
            <a href={`${base}systems/maya/`}>Maya page</a> explains the distinction from calendar
            place values.
          </p>
          <a class="bases-source" href="https://maya.nmai.si.edu/maya-sun/maya-math-game">
            Smithsonian · Maya mathematics ↗
          </a>
        </article>
        <article>
          <span class="bases-culture-number" aria-hidden="true">
            60
          </span>
          <h3>Sexagesimal · convenient shares</h3>
          <p>
            Babylonian scribes inherited sexagesimal traditions from the Sumerians. The original
            reason for sixty remains uncertain. Its many divisors do make sharing convenient: a
            half, a third, and a quarter are 30, 20, and 15 sixtieths. Usefulness is not proof of
            origin.
          </p>
          <p>
            Familiar traces remain in sixty minutes to an hour and sixty seconds to a minute.
            Historical cuneiform readings also depended on context to establish scale.
          </p>
          <a
            class="bases-source"
            href="https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/HistTopics/Babylonian_numerals/"
          >
            MacTutor · Babylonian numerals ↗
          </a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="bases-section" id="binary-hex" aria-labelledby="computer-heading">
      <div class="bases-section-intro">
        <span class="eyebrow bases-kicker">04 · FROM CIRCUITS TO READABLE DIGITS</span>
        <h2 id="computer-heading">Why computers use binary, and people use hex.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="bases-computer-grid">
        <div class="bases-computer-copy">
          <h3>Binary · two states</h3>
          <p>
            Digital circuits can represent two distinguishable states, such as low and high voltage,
            as 0 and 1. Designing around two separated states makes reliable switching practical.
            Each binary digit is a <strong>bit</strong>. Several bits together can represent
            numbers, letters, colors, instructions, and other information.
          </p>
          <a class="bases-source" href="https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/qubit">
            IBM · Bits and their physical representation ↗
          </a>
          <h3>Hexadecimal · four bits in one digit</h3>
          <p>
            Long binary strings are hard to read. Since 16 = 2⁴, exactly four bits fit into one
            hexadecimal digit. Hex uses 0–9, then{' '}
            <strong>A = 10, B = 11, C = 12, D = 13, E = 14, F = 15</strong>. After F comes 10,
            meaning one sixteen and zero ones.
          </p>
          <p>
            Hex is a compact way for people to read binary data, used in places such as color codes
            and memory addresses. It does not require a different kind of sixteen-state computer
            hardware.
          </p>
          <a class="bases-source" href="https://spaceplace.nasa.gov/binary-code3/en/">
            NASA · Why hexadecimal fits binary ↗
          </a>
        </div>
        <div class="bases-hex-reference">
          <h3>The sixteen possible four-bit groups</h3>
          <div
            class="base-table-scroll"
            tabindex="0"
            role="region"
            aria-label="Hexadecimal digit reference"
          >
            <table class="base-hex-table">
              <caption>One hex digit is exactly four binary bits</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr>
                  <th scope="col">Decimal</th>
                  <th scope="col">Hex</th>
                  <th scope="col">Binary</th>
                </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                {hexDigits.map((digit) => (
                  <tr>
                    <td>{digit.value}</td>
                    <th scope="row">{digit.hex}</th>
                    <td>{digit.bits}</td>
                  </tr>
                ))}
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="base-byte-example">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">A SHORTCUT BETWEEN BINARY AND HEX</span>
          <h3>10101101₂ = AD₁₆ = 173₁₀</h3>
          <p>
            Group binary digits in fours from the right. Add leading zeros to the leftmost group if
            needed; they do not change the value. Match each group to one hex digit. To go back,
            replace each hex digit with its four bits. Eight bits make one byte.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div
          class="base-byte-groups"
          role="img"
          aria-label="Binary 1010 is hexadecimal A; binary 1101 is hexadecimal D"
        >
          {byteExample.map((group) => (
            <div>
              <span>{group.binary}</span>
              <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
              <strong>{group.hex}</strong>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <aside class="bases-next">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow bases-kicker">TAKE THE NEXT STEP</span>
        <h2>How did people calculate?</h2>
        <p>Explore a civilization, select an operator, and follow its arithmetic method.</p>
      </div>
      <a href={`${base}#converter`}>
        Open the calculator <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </aside>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  import '../lib/bases-client';
</script>
